    {"source_id":"D011-FAMA-MACBETH-1973","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"University of Chicago Press, Journal of Political Economy","title":"Risk, Return, and Equilibrium: Empirical Tests","url":"https://www.journals.uchicago.edu/doi/10.1086/260061","source_family":"peer-reviewed asset-pricing methodology / foundational empirical research","locator":"Fama and MacBeth (1973), Journal of Political Economy 81(3), pp. 607â€“636; publisher record and abstract inspected 2026-10-23. Full text is subscriber-gated.","supports":"Foundational two-pass empirical test of the relationship between average stock returns and risk, providing context for the later named Fama-MacBeth cross-sectional estimation procedure in asset-pricing research.","claim_limit":"Historical US-equity study of specific equilibrium hypotheses; citation does not establish that the estimator is suitable for every panel, asset-pricing question, market or current model.","search_status":"publisher record/abstract opened 2026-10-23; full text gated"},
    {"source_id":"D011-CHERNOZHUKOV-DML-2018","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"Oxford University Press, The Econometrics Journal","title":"Double/Debiased Machine Learning for Treatment and Structural Parameters","url":"https://academic.oup.com/ectj/article/21/1/C1/5056401?login=false","source_family":"peer-reviewed econometric methods research","locator":"Chernozhukov et al. (2018), The Econometrics Journal 21(1), C1â€“C68; Oxford Academic article record/summary inspected 2026-10-23.","supports":"A principled bridge from modern ML to econometric inference: flexible learners estimate high-dimensional nuisance functions, while Neyman-orthogonal scores and cross-fitting target low-dimensional causal/structural parameters under stated conditions.","claim_limit":"DML does not create identification where assumptions fail; validity depends on the estimand, design, score, dependence structure, nuisance convergence and implementation. Ordinary supervised prediction, feature importance or black-box accuracy is not itself causal inference.","search_status":"publisher record and abstract inspected 2026-10-23"},
    {"source_id":"D011-ELGHOUL-INTERNATIONAL-EVENT-STUDY-2022","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"Springer Nature / Journal of International Business Studies; indexed by PubMed","title":"Event Studies in International Finance Research","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35822194/","source_family":"peer-reviewed international-finance methods review","locator":"El Ghoul, Guedhami, Mansi and Sy (2022), Journal of International Business Studies 53(2), pp. 344â€“364; PubMed record and indexed article abstract inspected 2026-10-23. Full text linked via PMC but direct fetch was bot-blocked.","supports":"Reviews event selection, event/estimation windows, normal-return models, abnormal-return inference and short- versus long-horizon challenges in cross-country equity and fixed-income research; emphasizes model and international-market heterogeneity.","claim_limit":"A literature review with recommendations, not a universal event-study protocol or causal identification guarantee. Long-horizon abnormal-return conclusions remain especially sensitive to expected-return model and market regime.","search_status":"official PubMed bibliographic/abstract record inspected 2026-10-23; PMC full text direct fetch blocked"},
