    {"source_id":"D011-FED-REALIZED-VOL-ML-COMPARE-2025-REV2026","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System, Finance and Economics Discussion Series","title":"Linear and Nonlinear Econometric Models versus Machine-Learning Models: Realized-Volatility Forecasting","url":"https://www.federalreserve.gov/econres/feds/linear-and-nonlinear-econometric-models-against-machine-learning-models.htm","source_family":"official central-bank working paper / credible grey literature","locator":"Rehim Kilic, FEDS 2025-061r1, August 2025, revised September 2026. Compares HAR, ARFIMA, threshold/smooth-transition/Markov-switching HAR with XGBoost and neural networks on S&P 500/40 U.S. equities; evaluates multiple horizons using MSFE, MAE, QLIKE, model-confidence sets, Dieboldâ€“Mariano tests, realized utility and VaR/ES. Results vary by horizon; ML sometimes improves HAR but does not systematically beat the broader econometric set.","supports":"Recent finance-specific comparison of traditional/extended econometric and machine-learning approaches to realized-volatility forecasting, including how long-memory, nonlinear and regime-dependent methods compare and why target horizon/evaluation criterion matter.","claim_limit":"Working paper, not Federal Reserve policy or a final consensus; U.S. equity samples and chosen predictor sets/horizons constrain transfer. The authors' stated disclaimer says findings are their own; recheck revision before relying on the result.","credibility_assessment":"High-value grey research source because it is a dated, versioned Federal Reserve working paper with a full methods/results paper, explicit markets, forecast protocols and multiple robustness/evaluation checks. It is preliminary author research rather than Board policy or peer-reviewed consensus; independently compare with peer-reviewed volatility research and other markets.","search_status":"official FEDS page and September 2026 revision metadata/abstract opened 2026-10-23; paper indexed, full analysis not independently replicated"},
    {"source_id":"D011-JEF-REALIZED-VOL-ML-2024","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"Journal of Empirical Finance / Elsevier","title":"Forecasting Realized Volatility: Does Anything Beat Linear Models?","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jempfin.2024.101524","source_family":"peer-reviewed finance research","locator":"Branco, Rubesam and Zevallos, Journal of Empirical Finance, September 2024. Compares linear and nonlinear ML models against HAR for ten global equity indices (2000â€“2021), at daily/weekly/monthly horizons; abstract reports no systematic ML outperformance, no single model dominates statistical/economic measures, and the preferred model varies with horizon/objective.","supports":"Independent peer-reviewed cross-market check against the Federal Reserve working paper; supports teaching HAR/linear baselines, ML as a tested alternative, and horizon/objective-specific comparison rather than automatic replacement.","claim_limit":"Ten indices and a bounded historical sample; abstract-level findings do not establish a universal ranking and detailed study design should be inspected before fine-grained route/technique claims.","search_status":"publisher article record and abstract inspected 2026-10-23; full text access restricted"},
    {"source_id":"D011-CHICAGO-FED-STOCHASTIC-VOLATILITY-2009","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"Federal Reserve Bank of Chicago","title":"Stochastic Volatility","url":"https://www.chicagofed.org/publications/working-papers/2009/wp-04","source_family":"central-bank research review / credible grey literature","locator":"Andersen and Benzoni, Chicago Fed Working Paper 2009-04. Distinguishes genuine stochastic-volatility models, where latent volatility has its own shocks, from observation-driven ARCH/GARCH specifications; discusses estimation/inference challenges and applications in derivatives, rates, FX and realized-volatility analysis.","supports":"Conceptual comparison of GARCH and latent stochastic-volatility model families and why model choice changes inference, computational approach and downstream pricing/risk tasks.","claim_limit":"A 2009 working-paper review, not a current implementation manual or evidence that one family is universally superior; use as conceptual framework alongside recent technical and empirical sources.","credibility_assessment":"Credible grey research from an identifiable Federal Reserve Bank working-paper series with named academic authors, date and traceable source citations. It is older and is author research, not central-bank policy; corroborate the conceptual distinctions and current estimation approaches with newer methods sources.","search_status":"official Chicago Fed working-paper page opened 2026-10-23"},
    {"source_id":"D011-JFUTURES-GARCH-IMPLIED-REALIZED-2016","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"Journal of Futures Markets / Wiley; manuscript hosted by University of Stirling repository","title":"Forecasting Stock Return Volatility: GARCH, Implied Volatility and Realized Volatility Models","url":"https://www.storre.stir.ac.uk/handle/1893/23189","source_family":"peer-reviewed financial econometrics / institutional repository grey access copy","locator":"Kambouroudis, McMillan and Tsakou (2016), Journal of Futures Markets 36(12), 1127â€“1163. Repository marks the article refereed and provides the accepted manuscript. Compares GARCH, option-implied, realized-volatility and combined ARMA/GARCH forecasts; combination results depend on the study and include VaR backtesting.","supports":"Shows that historical-return conditional variance, option-implied forward-looking measures and high-frequency realized measures are distinct information sources that can be compared or combined for risk forecasts; they are not simply interchangeable software alternatives.","claim_limit":"2016 stock-index study; results may differ by market, horizon, option liquidity, volatility proxy and loss/VaR criterion. Repository metadata and accepted manuscript should be checked against the final publisher version for citation precision.","credibility_assessment":"Credible grey access record for a peer-reviewed article: the university repository identifies the refereed journal/version and DOI and provides an accepted manuscript, while the journal is the version of record. The institutional host improves access but does not substitute for the publisher's final copy or broader replication.","search_status":"institutional repository record, referee status, DOI and abstract inspected 2026-10-23"},
    {"source_id":"D011-CORSI-HAR-RV-2009","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"Journal of Financial Econometrics / Oxford University Press","title":"A Simple Approximate Long-Memory Model of Realized Volatility","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/jjfinec/nbp001","source_family":"peer-reviewed financial econometrics research","locator":"Fulvio Corsi (2009), Journal of Financial Econometrics 7(2), 174â€“196. Proposes HAR-RV as a parsimonious heterogeneous daily/weekly/monthly realized-volatility cascade; demonstrates forecasting properties and long-memory-like empirical features without claiming a true long-memory process.","supports":"Origin/interpretation of HAR-RV and its relationship to horizon aggregation in realized-volatility modeling; a transparent econometric baseline for comparison with richer econometric and ML approaches.","claim_limit":"The model's approximation and reported sample results do not imply literal long memory or universal forecast superiority; inspect sampling/estimation details and compare under the learner's task and horizon.","search_status":"publisher DOI record and abstract cross-checked through academic index 2026-10-23"},
    {"source_id":"D011-NBER-REALIZED-VOL-2001","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"National Bureau of Economic Research","title":"Modeling and Forecasting Realized Volatility","url":"https://www.nber.org/papers/w8160","source_family":"academic working paper / credible grey literature","locator":"Andersen, Bollerslev, Diebold and Labys, NBER Working Paper 8160 (2001), subsequently published in Econometrica 71(2), 579â€“625 (2003). Links high-frequency realized variance to daily/lower-frequency forecasts; compares long-memory Gaussian VAR on realized volatility with daily ARCH models in FX data and discusses density/quantile forecasts.","supports":"Foundational bridge from high-frequency measurement to conventional time-series modeling and risk-distribution outputs; demonstrates that realized-volatility series can be modeled using AR/VAR-type methods rather than only ARCH/GARCH on returns.","claim_limit":"Older FX sample and period; the NBER working-paper record is a discovery/access source for a later journal article. Verify final published version for detailed technical claims and assess modern microstructure/noise corrections separately.","credibility_assessment":"Strong academic working-paper record with named authors, date, full citation, later peer-reviewed publication and a specific empirical design; the 2001 results are historical and market-bounded, so do not treat them as a current performance ranking.","search_status":"NBER record and published-version citation inspected 2026-10-23"},
