    {"source_id":"D011-BIS-CVA-CREDIT-VALUATION-FRAMEWORK","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"Basel Committee on Banking Supervision / Bank for International Settlements","title":"Basel Framework MAR50: Credit Valuation Adjustment Framework","url":"https://www.bis.org/committees/bcbs/basel-framework/standard/mar/50/inforce/2022-01-01/published/2019-12-15","source_family":"prudential regulatory framework","locator":"MAR50.3 and MAR50.32: CVA uses discounted future exposure, market-implied PD term structures estimated from traded credit spreads, and market-consensus expected LGD; illiquid counterparties may use peer-based proxy curves under rating/industry/region criteria. The framework distinguishes market-implied default probability from observed real-world likelihood.","supports":"A regulator-defined valuation/risk output that combines exposure simulation, discounting, credit spread/default-probability term structures and loss-given-default; shows why market-implied credit valuation is distinct from historical borrower default modeling.","claim_limit":"Basel prudential capital framework with jurisdictional adoption and in-force-version limits; it is not a universal derivative-pricing recipe or a claim that all CVA desks use identical models. Check the applicable current Basel implementation and local rules.","search_status":"BIS Basel Framework MAR50 sections and current-page metadata inspected 2026-10-23"},
