    {"source_id":"D011-LEDOIT-WOLF-SHRINKAGE-2004","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"Olivier Ledoit and Michael Wolf; Journal of Portfolio Management","title":"Honey, I Shrunk the Sample Covariance Matrix","url":"https://www.ledoit.net/honey_abstract.htm","source_family":"peer-reviewed portfolio research / author-hosted paper and code","locator":"Journal of Portfolio Management 30(4), 110â€“119 (2004). The authors compare the sample covariance with a data-driven linear shrinkage estimator and explain its effect on meanâ€“variance portfolio estimation error and realized portfolio performance.","supports":"Traditional baseline versus linear shrinkage for covariance inputs to meanâ€“variance portfolio construction; links covariance estimation to weight stability, tracking error and realized information ratio.","claim_limit":"A historical empirical study and one estimator/benchmark setting; its abstract is not a universal recommendation. Reassess assumptions, market period, target, loss function, constraints and modern alternatives before applying to a current portfolio.","search_status":"author paper page and journal citation inspected 2026-10-23"},
    {"source_id":"D011-LEDOIT-WOLF-NONLINEAR-SHRINKAGE-2012","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"Institute of Mathematical Statistics / The Annals of Statistics","title":"Nonlinear Shrinkage Estimation of Large-Dimensional Covariance Matrices","url":"https://doi.org/10.1214/12-AOS989","source_family":"peer-reviewed statistical methodology","locator":"The Annals of Statistics 40(2), 1024â€“1060 (2012), open access. Studies high-dimensional covariance estimation; contrasts linear shrinkage toward a common target with nonlinear eigenvalue shrinkage and evaluates estimation performance in theory and simulation.","supports":"A methodological extension beyond the 2004 linear-shrinkage family for large-dimensional covariance estimation, which can feed portfolio optimization and other inverse-covariance tasks.","claim_limit":"Asymptotic/statistical results and simulations do not by themselves prove superior realized performance for every financial universe, return process, constraint set or objective. Check dependence, nonstationarity and portfolio-level out-of-sample metrics.","search_status":"Project Euclid journal article and abstract inspected 2026-10-23"},
    {"source_id":"D011-LEDOIT-WOLF-QUADRATIC-SHRINKAGE-2022","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"Bernoulli Society / Bernoulli journal","title":"Quadratic Shrinkage for Large Covariance Matrices","url":"https://doi.org/10.3150/20-BEJ1315","source_family":"peer-reviewed statistical methodology","locator":"Bernoulli 28(3), 1519â€“1547 (2022), open access. Proposes quadratic shrinkage of inverse sample eigenvalues, relates finite-sample Stein estimation to large-dimensional asymptotics, and compares with linear shrinkage under stated loss and simulation settings.","supports":"A later covariance/inverse-covariance estimator family and alternative to linear/nonlinear eigenvalue shrinkage for high-dimensional risk estimation and optimization.","claim_limit":"The paper's asymptotic loss, simulations and assumptions do not establish universal portfolio superiority or suitability under every dependence, regime, constraint or transaction-cost setting.","search_status":"journal record and abstract inspected 2026-10-23"},
    {"source_id":"D011-ELKAROUI-LIM-ML-PORTFOLIO-2018","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"INFORMS / Management Science","title":"Machine Learning and Portfolio Optimization","url":"https://pubsonline.informs.org/doi/10.1287/mnsc.2016.2644","source_family":"peer-reviewed finance and operations research","locator":"Management Science 64(3), 1136â€“1154 (2018), published online 2016. Introduces performance-based regularization and cross-validation for meanâ€“variance and mean-CVaR optimization, compares with sample-average, L1/L2 regularization and equal weighting on three Famaâ€“French datasets.","supports":"Shows how statistical learning (regularization and cross-validation) can be applied to portfolio decisions and estimation risk, rather than treating ML as a separate forecasting-only stage; links covariance/return estimates to portfolio-level out-of-sample objectives.","claim_limit":"Empirical comparison covers particular datasets, formulations and benchmarks; results do not guarantee transfer to other markets, objectives, costs, regimes or live trading. The paper's proposed performance-based methods require independent reproduction and leakage-aware validation.","search_status":"INFORMS article abstract, publication metadata and study scope inspected 2026-10-23"},
    {"source_id":"D011-CONLON-COTTER-KYNIGAKIS-FACTOR-COVARIANCE-2025","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"European Journal of Operational Research / Elsevier","title":"Asset Allocation with Factor-Based Covariance Matrices","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0377221725002127","source_family":"peer-reviewed portfolio and operations research","locator":"European Journal of Operational Research 325(1), 189â€“203 (2025). Compares factor-based covariance approaches, including PCA/PLS-derived latent factors and static/dynamic structures, for minimum-variance portfolios; reports risk-adjusted results after transaction costs within the study design.","supports":"Factor covariance as a lower-dimensional alternative to unconstrained sample covariance, with ML dimensionality-reduction approaches as candidate factor-construction methods; shows why estimation and portfolio results need cost-aware evaluation.","claim_limit":"One empirical study with specific assets, data, benchmarks and transaction-cost assumptions; its results do not establish general outperformance or universal suitability. Distinguish factor exposure modeling from ML forecasting and validate on the intended universe and period.","search_status":"journal article abstract, methods overview and study limitations inspected 2026-10-23"},
    {"source_id":"D011-GOLDBERG-GURDOGAN-KERCHEVAL-EIGENVECTOR-SHRINKAGE-2025","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"Finance and Stochastics / Springer Nature","title":"Portfolio Optimisation via Strategy-Specific Eigenvector Shrinkage","url":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00780-025-00566-4","source_family":"peer-reviewed quantitative-finance research","locator":"Finance and Stochastics 29, 665â€“706 (2025), open access. Develops a generalized Jamesâ€“Stein estimator for leading sample eigenvectors, using portfolio-constraint gradients to define a target subspace, and studies minimum-variance portfolios with finitely many linear constraints.","supports":"A modern estimator that makes covariance estimation strategy/constraint-aware, complementing eigenvalue shrinkage and generic factor structure when the downstream task is constrained minimum-variance allocation.","claim_limit":"A recent theoretical and empirical contribution tied to stated linear constraints and model conditions; not a general covariance estimator or evidence that the approach wins for arbitrary mandates. Requires replication and comparison to relevant baselines.","search_status":"journal article metadata and abstract inspected 2026-10-23"},
    {"source_id":"D011-MATTERA-RL-COVARIANCE-SHRINKAGE-2023","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"Elsevier / Intelligent Systems with Applications","title":"Shrinkage Estimation with Reinforcement Learning of Large Variance Matrices for Portfolio Selection","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2667305323000066","source_family":"peer-reviewed machine-learning and portfolio research","locator":"Intelligent Systems with Applications 17 (2023), article 200181. Proposes policy-gradient and recurrent-policy-gradient agents to tune shrinkage intensity for large covariance matrices, with an empirical asset-allocation comparison on industry portfolios.","supports":"An emerging ML-based way to adapt a traditional shrinkage parameter; the method operates inside covariance estimation and portfolio selection rather than replacing the underlying risk/optimization problem.","claim_limit":"A single proposed method and bounded empirical comparison; it does not establish robust superiority, safe deployment or transfer across markets. Compare with transparent linear/nonlinear shrinkage and simple baselines using time-aware validation and transaction costs.","search_status":"journal record, abstract and study-design summary inspected 2026-10-23"},
    {"source_id":"D011-NEURAL-NONLINEAR-SHRINKAGE-2026","domain_id":"D011","publisher":"Oxford University Press / Journal of Financial Econometrics","title":"Learning the Shrinkage Intensity: A Data-Driven Approach for Risk-Optimized Portfolios","url":"https://academic.oup.com/jfec/article/24/2/nbag002/8512976","source_family":"peer-reviewed financial econometrics and machine learning","locator":"Journal of Financial Econometrics 24(2), online 2026. Studies supervised policy learning for data-dependent covariance shrinkage intensities in high-dimensional minimum-variance portfolios; situates the contribution beside linear/nonlinear shrinkage and reinforcement-learning approaches and evaluates out-of-sample portfolio risk.","supports":"A current ML extension that learns/adapts covariance-shrinkage intensity for a portfolio-risk objective; preserves the covariance-estimation-to-portfolio-output context and makes the learned policy itself a model to validate.","claim_limit":"Very recent paper and empirical design; the abstract's reported gains are author-reported and do not prove future/live-market superiority. Inspect full methods/data, benchmark leakage and regime assumptions, and independently replicate before making a student-facing performance claim.","search_status":"journal page and abstract inspected 2026-10-23"},
